Log File Analysis for Incident Detection

tep 1: Navigate to Log Directory
cd /var/log
ls
Observation:
You will see files such as:
dpkg.log → package activity
apache2/ → web logs
nginx/ → web logs
journal/ → system logs
wtmp, btmp → login records

Step 2: View Successful Login History
last
Purpose:
Displays successful login sessions
Helps identify unusual login times

Step 3: View Failed Login Attempts
sudo lastb
Purpose:
Shows failed login attempts
Useful for detecting brute force attacks

Step 4: Analyze System Logs
journalctl | less
Purpose:
View full system logs
Includes services, authentication, errors

Step 5: Filter Failed Login Attempts
journalctl | grep "Failed password"
Purpose:
Detect authentication failures
Identify possible attack attempts

Step 6: View SSH Activity
journalctl | grep ssh
Purpose:
Shows SSH login attempts
Tracks remote access activity

Step 7: Detect Errors
journalctl | grep -i error
Purpose:
Identify abnormal system behavior
Detect issues or attacks

Step 8: Analyze Apache Logs (Web Activity)
cd /var/log/apache2
ls
Open log file:
sudo less access.log
Purpose:
Monitor web requests
Detect suspicious browsing behavior

Step 9: Detect Suspicious Web Requests
grep "404" /var/log/apache2/access.log
Purpose:
Identify failed page requests
Indicates scanning or probing

Step 10: Check Package Activity
less /var/log/dpkg.log
Purpose:
Detect unauthorized installations
Track system changes

Step 11: Real-Time Log Monitoring
sudo journalctl -f
Purpose:
Monitor logs live
Detect incidents instantly

5. Attack Simulation (IMPORTANT PART)

Step 12: Install SSH Server (if not installed)
sudo apt update
sudo apt install openssh-server -y

Step 13: Start SSH Service
sudo service ssh start

Step 14: Find IP Address
ip a
Look for:
inet 192.168.x.x

Step 15: Generate Failed Login Attempts
ssh fakeuser@localhost
OR
ssh kali@localhost
👉 Enter wrong password 5–10 times

Step 16: Detect Failed Logins
journalctl | grep "Failed password"
Output Example:
Failed password for kali from 127.0.0.1 port 22

Step 17: Extract Suspicious IP Address
journalctl | grep "Failed password" | awk '{print $11}'
Output:
127.0.0.1

Step 18: Count Number of Attempts
journalctl | grep "Failed password" | awk '{print $11}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr
Example Output:
10 127.0.0.1

Step 19: Analyze Recent Activity
journalctl --since "10 minutes ago"
Purpose:
View recent suspicious events

6. Analysis of Observed Logs
✔ Normal Activities:
System services (systemd, cleanup services)
CRON jobs (scheduled tasks)
User sudo commands
GUI services
⚠ Abnormal but Not Attack:
Repeated VirtualBox messages
Indicates system/VM issue (not hacking)
🚨 Suspicious Activity:
Multiple failed login attempts
Same IP repeated
Indicates brute force attack

7. Result
Successfully analyzed system logs
Detected failed SSH login attempts
Identified suspicious IP address
Observed normal vs abnormal system behavior
